at this was a result of the tenth-century Byzantine victories in the east and the end of Byzantine persecution of Jews.”\r\n\r\n"Imperial expansion in the east [in the tenth century] also brought more Jewish communities into the empire. Also, the intolerant climate of Fatimid Egypt led many Jews (as well as Melkites) to migrate to Romanía, which was ﬂourishing economically. The empire was now crisscrossed by denser Jewish networks that extended throughout the eastern Mediterranean and into Muslim-ruled lands. Overall, these Jewish communities appear to have ﬂourished too."'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 926, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 792})
