 of the extended royal family. (pers.comm. 2025)\r\n\r\n“The ‘Land of Luwiya’ appears to be an Old Hittite ethno-linguistic term referring to the area where Luwain was spoken, and also the ‘Land of Pala’ with its languages Palaic (Palaumnili). That the two terms occur together in the Old Hittite Laws opposed to the ‘Land of Hatti’ (Hattusa), the Hittite homeland, seems to show recognition on the part of the Hittites of kinship based on language, and it also seems likely that Luwiya and Pala were adjacent to Hatti.”  “They [Paphlagonians] occupied the mountainous coastal region lying between Bithynia and the Black Sea called Pala (combined with Tummanna) in the second millennium. The language spoken by its inhabitants, Palaic, was one of three Indo-European languages (along with Nesite and Luwian) introduced into Anatolia probably during the course of the third millennium. Lying to the north-west of the Hittite homeland, the Palaians became attached to the Hittite kingdom as a vassal state. Pala occupied an important strategic location from the Hittite point of view, near the Kaska zone and serving as a kind of north-western buffer zone for the Hittite homeland.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1152, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1021})
