 witnessing a ‘florescence… unrivalled anywhere else in the medieval Islamic world’. Its classic pencil-thin form is characteristically Seljuk. The same process of institutionalisation affected other aspects of Islam, with the spread of madrasa and the emergence of its Shi’ite relative, the hawza, the seminary system based in the Iraqi cities of Najaf and al-Hilla. Developments in Sufism also left their imprint on the built landscape. As Sufism became ever more mainstream as it was reconciled with theology, and increasingly institutionalised, some Sufi disciples started to live temporarily or permanently in specific buildings aside for their use, variously called Khanqah, ribat or zawiya, which would often also sever as the burial place for holy men (awliya, sing. wali). Meanwhile, the Sevener Shi’ite Nizari Ismailis developed their own theology, ideology and state in their remote mountain hideouts within the very confines of the Seljuk Empire.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1029, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 893})
