se the range and force of a projectile, absent or present as a 'military technology during this time? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": "The largest ethnic group in Ethiopia traditionally followed their own religion and later often converted to Christianity and Islam. They migrated in large numbers to the Christian highlands of Ethiopia in the sixteenth century and are often mentioned by Christian sources in a derogatory way (Gebissa 2010)." "The Oromo surrounding Harar were forced to convert to Islam by the Turco-Egyptian colonial forces between 1875 and 1885."  “During the rule of Emir Muhammad (1856-1875), the influence of the Oromo in Harar grew. They were further absorbed into Islam through close dealings with the Harari […] Consequently, the Oromo like the Somali in the region, underwent major social and cultural changes. The pattern of life which was governed by strict Islamic rules took place to a large extent within village communities, some of which developed into important markets as a result of contact with Harari and Arab traders.” […] “As mentioned before, Islam spread from the Emirate of Harar across Southern and Eastern Ethiopia and this was how the Oromo in Hararghe, including those living in Babile first experienced its influence.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 924, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 819})
