n interpreted as stylised representations of bulls’ heads, and it has been argued that they may be connected with religious practices analogous to those of the recent Kunama in western Eritrea. Archaeological evidence from the Ancient Ona site of Sembel Kushet has been interpreted as representing activity comparable with that continued into modern times at the Masqal celebrations of the Eritrean and Ethiopian Orthodox Churches."<br>NB Perhaps better classified as a very small minority instead; provisionally coded as a sizeable minority based on the evidence that there may have been Egyptian communities in three major cities. "The newly discovered stele at Aksum, with the Egyptian symbol of life ankh, and objects pertaining to the cult of Hathor, Ptah and Horus, as well as scarabs, suggests that adherents to the Egyptian-Meroe religion were residing at some time in Aksum, Adulis and Matara."'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 998, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 869})
