= absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": “Together with writing, the Sogdians also brought Buddhism. Türk Buddhism was, in its oldest stratum, under Sogdian and Chinese influence. The Bugut inscription shows that Buddhism was present in the empire at the time of the first sovereigns. Maniakh, the ambassador of the Türks at Constantinople, bore a Buddhist name and his family seems to have been well established at the court of Sizabul, since his son was raised there and while still young was given the second rank in a second Türk embassy: everything therefore indicates that this Sogdian family was strongly integrated into the framework of the Türk hierarchy, where it possessed rank and hereditary titles even though the Türk Empire had only just been created."\xa0 “Tardu\'s rule extended over a long period of time (he died in 603) and over a vast tract of land comprising the Western Frontier Region of the Turk empire, authority over which lay, theoretically at least, in the hands of Bumin\'s second son Muhan (553-72) and, following his death, in those of Muhan\'s younger brother Taspar (572-81). Their names appear in the Sogdian inscription of Bugut, which testifies to the abiding role of the Sogdians within the empire and also to the presence of Buddhism in their midst. […] It is clear that, besides Buddhism and Zoroastrianism (reported by Theophylactus Simocattes), Nestorian Christianity was also known to and practiced by at least some Türks.[…] Although present, Zoroastrianism, Christianity, and Manicheism (mainly among Sogdians) played minor roles compared with Buddhism.”  “The religious beliefs of the Türk focused on a sky god, Tängri, and an earth goddess, Umay. Some of the Turks—notably the Western Turks in Tokharistan—converted very early to Buddhism, and it played an important role among them. Other religions were also influential, particularly Christianity and Manichaeism, which were popular among the Sogdians, close allies of the Türk who were skilled in international trade. Although the Sogdians were a settled, urban people, they were like the Türk in that they also had a Central Eurasian warrior ethos with a pervasive comitatus tradition, and both peoples were intensely interested in trade.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1159, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1075})
