ally in Bungo, even as the storm gathered over the domain of the Otomo: There were 2,000 adult baptisms in 1584, no fewer than 12,000 in 1585 (almost half that number in Funai and its vicinity alone), and 6,150 in the first nine months of 1586. To note that is by no means to suggest that these Christians posed a threat to the Otomo because they were some- how disloyal or even seditious. But because many important vassals of the Otomo continued to cherish traditional religion, it is plain that the issue of the toleration - let alone the advancement - of the Christian cause by the daimyo family was highly divisive at the best of times and downright explosive during a time of crisis." <br>“After Xavier’s departure the influence of the Kirishitan steadily increased until their numerical strength was estimated to be about 30,000 in 1570.” “By 1579, when a leading daimyo in northern Kyushu accepted Christianity, eight thousand people, a third of his domain’s population- followed the same faith. By 1582, Jesuits claimed 150,000 converts and two hundred churches in Japan. Warrior leaders ignored this growth until the 1580s when they decided Christianity posed a threat […].” “By the time Nobunaga was assassinated, 150,000 Japanese Catholics, including several daimyo, were reported to be among the Japanese population.” “'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1133, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1048})
