venna  in  the  north.  The  size  of  these  communities  is  dif-ficult  to  estimate  given  the  limitations  of  ancient  demography.  However,  it  does  appear  that  the  urban  population,  especially  in  Rome  itself,  expanded  throughout the late antique period as Jews increasingly moved to cities from smaller rural areas of Italy.” <br>“Ostrogothic Italy was comprised of different religious communities, the most prominent of which were Catholic Christians, Jews, and the Ostrogoths themselves who have typically been labelled as ‘Arians’.”  “There  had  been  Jewish  communities  in  Italy  for  centuries  before  the  advent  of  the  Ostrogothic  kingdom;  however  it  was  in  Late  Antiquity  that  Italian  Jewry  became,  in  the  words  of  Leonard  Rutgers,  “the  single  most  visible  and  tangible  Jewish  community  of  the  entire  western  Diaspora”.  The  largest  and  oldest  Jewish  centre  was  in  Rome.5  But  other  important  Jewish  communi-ties  included  Palermo  and  Catania  in  Sicily,  Venosa  and  Naples  in  the  south,  and  Milan  and  Ravenna  in  the  north.  The  size  of  these  communities  is  dif-ficult  to  estimate  given  the  limitations  of  ancient  demography.  However,  it  does  appear  that  the  urban  population,  especially  in  Rome  itself,  expanded  throughout the late antique period as Jews increasingly moved to cities from smaller rural areas of Italy.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1288, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1204})
