r', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": “The French were not the first to challenge the Portuguese padroado: when Nicolas Trigualt, the procurator for the Jesuit China missions in 1615, pushed a little too hard for recruiting more non-Portuguese than Portuguese Jesuits, he was accused of ‘breaching’ the royal rights, ‘anti-Portuguese parochialism,’ and ‘pan-European Internationalism.’ The debate on the legality of such actions and more specifically Louis XIV’s support is not straight forward as the (proto-nationalistic) Portuguese side experienced it: throughout the 17TH century the Papacy itself tried ‘to erode the [rules regarding] the padroado by declaring that the padroado rights did not apply in ‘estra dominium temporale regni Portugallie, beyond where the crown enjoys actual control (‘temporal dominion’)- thus extending independent kingdoms in Asia and Africa. An example of this was Pope Urban VIII’s bull in 1633: it stated that all orders and congregations had the right to establish missions in China and Japan, and that the missionaries could travel to those regions by whatever way they wanted.”  “Fantasy of a Chinese Constantine notwithstanding, the Edict of 1692 inaugurated the golden years of the Catholic mission in China for more than a dozen years. Imperial favours multiplied, the numbers of converts grew, and more Jesuits entered the Qing Empire. All these positive developments occurred with the newly arrived French Jesuits were embroiled in a bitter dispute with their Portuguese conferes over the establishment on an independent French Jesuit mission, subject only to their superior in France and Louis XIV, and not to the Jesuit Vice-Province of China that stood under Portuguese protection.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1020, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 941})
