ce, and each deity was acknowledged as having its own specific laws, the Aro-Chukwu oracle was a respected source of guidance and religious inspiration.”  “Throughout Igboland, the ubiquitous Aro agents of the oracle initiated a variety of commercial and ritual uniformity that essentially connected the communities especially in central Igbo areas in a bond of religious affinity, just as the Nri did in the Igbo areas close to the Niger and Anambra rivers.” <br>“The southwestern kingdom of Aro was significant in the seventeenth through nineteenth centuries, renowned for being the only location of a shrine devoted exclusively to Chukwu. As a southeastern Igbo kingdom closest to the southern coast, it became an important commercial intermediary, but, more important, it functioned as an oracle that offered judgement about the guilt or innocence of those suspected of committing crimes. Although each tribe of Igboland instantiated its own form of social organization and procedures for ensuring social order and administering justice, and each deity was acknowledged as having its own specific laws, the Aro-Chukwu oracle was a respected source of guidance and religious inspiration. One finds, then a connection between Igbo religion (Aro-Chukwu) and Igbo morality in the use of an oracle to make decisions and also the development of economic forms of social exchange.”  “Throughout Igboland, the ubiquitous Aro agents of the oracle initiated a variety of commercial and ritual uniformity that essentially connected the communities especially in central Igbo areas in a bond of religious affinity, just as the Nri did in the Igbo areas close to the Niger and Anambra rivers.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1136, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1020})
