wing device used to increase the range and force of a projectile, absent or present as a 'military technology during this time? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": “Since the farthest reaches of collective memory, the Oyo-Yoruba have recognized the existence of a high god, Olorun, and numerous deities called Orisa (primordial beings, deified ancestors and personification of natural phenomena such as rivers, hills or mountains), (NO-often considered ancient ancestors or heroes.)”  NB in the 15th-17th centuries, the Old Oyo Empire expanded into the territory of Igbomina to the north of Oyo. “The smallest site, Egudupo, was a special activity site, containing a public shrine for Egudu, an important ancient cult object associated with the chiefly elite in Ipo.” […] “Igbominaland sites provide interesting evidence of iron working in the form of quarries, slag mounds, smithing workshops, smelting furnaces, and shrines linked to Ogun, the god of iron in the Yoruba pantheon.” […] “Evidence of ritual that probably functioned to legitimize local chiefly authority is found at some sites. Egudupo was a public shrine associated with the Olupo kingship institutions, located in the heart of the Ipo settlements with close affinity to large elite centers like Gbagede and Okegi. Local people claim that an iron chain which Olupo brought from Old Oyo is buried at the site. In the past, the cult object, orisa egudu was kept in the settlement where the chief resided but was brought to this shrine every year for ritual ceremonies […] The practice of transporting a religious object from a shrine reflects the general practice in Yorubaland where items of worship except for coweries and pots used to receive offerings, were seldom left behind.”<br>'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1075, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 939})
