pear-throwing device used to increase the range and force of a projectile, absent or present as a 'military technology during this time? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": NB the following quote does not explicitly state that it was the most widespread religion, but it does suggest that Theravada Buddhism was on the rise at this time. \'With the fading of the ANGKOR era, Cambodia shifts capitals south to Lovek, then to Udong, and finally to PHNOM PENH.This period also sees the waning of HINDUISM and the rise of THERAVADA BUDDHISM.\' The following quote confirms Buddhist resurgence in this period, again mentioning the Theravada sect. “A general resurgence of Buddhist activity is confirmed by the accounts of European adventurers […] one of them asserted that a third of the male population were Buddhist. This must be a massive overestimate, but the report of sizable monastic estates (B.-P. Groslier 1958, 160) suggests that institutional Buddhism was prospering. […] The process of buddhification was not confined to Angkor Wat. Many early Theravada monastic sites emerged through the conversion of preexisting structures.” <br>\r\n\r\nRegarding the question of perhaps non-karmic traditions being more widespread among commoners? Expert, David Kyle Latinis stated:" Yes and No: \r\nThe ancestor and other more mythical placations I mention (i.e., “neak ta”). However, like karma, there are positive and negative consequences – if you boil down karma to its essence: reward and punishment. Unlike Ibrahamic religions, however, the sense of reincarnation may mean that the positive or negative results play out in future lifetimes, eventually enlightenment, nirvana, etc."\r\n\r\nThe following quote suggests that contemporary reports stating that a third of the male population was Buddhist were likely exaggerated, which in turn suggests that Theravada Buddhists may have been a sizeable minority. “A general resurgence of Buddhist activity is confirmed by the accounts of European adventurers […] one of them asserted that a third of the male population were Buddhist. This must be a massive overestimate, but the report of sizable monastic estates (B.-P. Groslier 1958, 160) suggests that institutional Buddhism was prospering. […] The process of buddhification was not confined to Angkor Wat. Many early Theravada monastic sites emerged through the conversion of preexisting structures.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1178, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1054})
