t from the mid-Qasim Dynasty onwards, Zaydism (including Hadawi teachings) fell out of favour and Qasimis turned to Sunni Islam: “The expansion of the Qasimi state into Sunni areas led to unprecedented levels of interaction between Zaydi and Shafi\'i scholars. The effect was that Zaydis acquired greater awarness of the wider Sunni world and began studying Sunni works, namely the collections of hadlth, with an intensity never before seen, and some even adopted Sunni views.” <br>NB dates are approximative, as evidence in the literature consulted was mixed. Of the following quotes, the first quote suggests that in the early days of the Qasimi Dynasty, Zaydi Islam was the predominantly practised religion: “The Qasimi Dynasty was born in the midst of Zaydi messianic expectations and Imam al-Mutawakkil clearly meant to exploit if not fulfil, the expectations. If his father had been the great deliverer and his brother the vanquisher of the Ottomans, he was the re-creator of the ideal Muslim community; his efforts to emulate the Prophet Muhammed are noteworthy in this regard”  This second and third quotes suggest that from the mid-Qasimi Dynasty onwards, over half of the population now practised Shafi’i (Sunni) Islam: "However, some Zaydi-born scholars even went beyond according Shafi\'is equal religious status with Zaydis. They did so by identifying more fully with Sunnism and forgoing many Zaydi teachings.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1151, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1069})
