y choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": Official documents suggest an overwhelming majority of Sakha had converted to Orthodoxy, but in daily life Christian institutions would have played an extremely minimal role for most. Post-Russian contact, syncretism characterizes religious practice of the Sakha.  “Yakut religion derives from Turkic, Mongolic, Tungusic, and Russian ideas. Labels like “animist,” “shamanist” or “Russian Orthodox” do not suffice. Ideas of sin are syncretised with concepts of contamination and taboo. Saints and bears are seen as shamanic spirit helpers. Christ is identified with the Yakut Bright Creator Elder God, Aiyy-toyon.”  "The events described took place in the second half of the 19th century, when an extensive network of diocesan churches was already operating in Yakutia. According to the First General Census of the Population In the Russian Empire, more than 98% of the region\'s population professed Orthodoxy. But the census recorded the official religious belonging… Information about shamans, as a rule, did not leave the limits of the diocese. Traveling priests testify to the systematic activity of shamans.” (Rough translation of Russian text) <br>As mentioned in the above quotation, officially the vast majority (98%) were Orthodox, however, in practice Shamanistic rituals retained their importance in daily life. Several of the quotations in the syncretism section will better illustrate what this hybrid version of Orthodoxy and Shamanism looked like in practice.  "By the end of the 18th century, the majority of the population of the Yakutsk Territory were converted to the new Orthodox faith, but then, and later, the local population was characterized by syncretism." (Rough translation of Russian text) '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1040, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 955})
