nd force of a projectile, absent or present as a 'military technology during this time? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": “The fact still remains that the provinces were conceived of and treated as part of the Asante itself. The Asante empire, as a political structure, had a cultural foundation, because of the different Akan peoples had cultural affiliations and an identity with each other. Again, before the 1896 rule, it was Asante that provided the Akan with the opportunity to come together as a single nation. The provincial states of the Asante kingdom were culturally homogeneous whilst the tributary states were culturally heterogeneous […] The cultural homogeneity of the provincial Akan state, was characterised by common beliefs and similar religious values expressed in their festivals.”  “Well before the arrival of Europeans and their Christian religion, the Akan developed a belief in a supreme, omnipresent being. The Asante believe in a single God that created everything in the universe, including the lesser deities. They call the supreme god ‘Nyame’. The creator is referred to by using many titles: the Great One, the Great Spirit, the Great Ancestor, Omnipotent, Infinite, and so on. The spiritual presence of Nyame is in all things. As such, the Asante venerate the spiritual presence in rivers, trees, rocks, and so on. However, they do not worship those objects, but only the spiritual entities that use those objects as their abode.”  “Islam as a religion remained marginal to the majority of indigenous Asante people in terms of conversion. In the interior of the empire, people still remained committed to their indigenous religions and other cultural practices, which were deeply oriented to the local customs and ways of thinking.” <br>The following quotes suggest that, in this period, most Akan followed their own traditional beliefs. “Well before the arrival of Europeans and their Christian religion, the Akan developed a belief in a supreme, omnipresent being. The Asante believe in a single God that created everything in the universe, including the lesser deities. They call the supreme god ‘Nyame’. The creator is referred to by using many titles: the Great One, the Great Spirit, the Great Ancestor, Omnipotent, Infinite, and so on. The spiritual presence of Nyame is in all things. As such, the Asante venerate the spiritual presence in rivers, trees, rocks, and so on. However, they do not worship those objects, but only the spiritual entities that use those objects as their abode.”  “Islam as a religion remained marginal to the majority of indigenous Asante people in terms of conversion. In the interior of the empire, people still remained committed to their indigenous religions and other cultural practices, which were deeply oriented to the local customs and ways of thinking.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1256, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1136})
