sed to increase the range and force of a projectile, absent or present as a 'military technology during this time? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": "Religious Beliefs. As in previous periods, there was great effort expended on mortuary ritual by kin groups of both high and low status, indicating pervasive beliefs about an afterlife and the importance of ancestors. Religious Practitioners. Artifactual and textual data (from later periods) suggest to some scholars that shamans were important religious specialists during the late Neolithic period. Ceremonies. It is likely, as in earlier periods, that funerals were important public rituals. Unusual artifacts, including alligator-skin drums and chime-stones, at sites such as Taosi in Shanxi suggest the importance of music in rituals."  "It also is clear that divination was widespread. Burned, but not incised, oracle bones, mostly the scapula from pig, ox, sheep, and deer, were recovered from two sites north of the Songshan mountains, the large site of Miaodian, and the smaller site of Xiaopangou. They also were found at two major sites in the south: the large site of Wadian and the walled site of Haojiatai. One ox scapula found at Xiaopangou had more than 30 heating marks, presumably made for divination. "Finally, earlier and simpler forms of rituals known in the later historical period seem to have begun during the Wangwan III period. A good example is the pottery lie gu 列觚, a ranked set of gu 觚 goblets excavated at the large site of Wadian. This was a set of goblets of decreasing sizes similar to the lie ding 列鼎 sets of tripods found in later Shang and Zhou dynasty contexts." '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1059, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 933})
