s time? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": “Excavations revealed that the basic structures still can be seen high up in the western part of within the fortress, as well as the fortress itself, the corridor; this might have been the site of were associated with religious ceremonies. […] Top of the east wall easily could have accommodated a narrow gallery. In the mass of this wall, near the spot where it adjoins the fortress, a good-sized, carefully filled-in niche set high above the level of daily activities was discovered. Possibly it was associated with religious ceremonies, for the construction of the bottom (floor) of the niche indicates that a large object could have been fastened to it. The walls of the entrance complex had neither galleries nor loopholes for archers. Even today, within the walls there is an amazing acoustic resonance. The ancient inhabitants may have taken advantage of this in their rites. […] there were cult niches in the western segment of the fortress wall sheltered by overhangs. […] It may be assumed that the ritual center of Kalali-gir 2, which could communicate with almost all of the southern portion of the near side of the Sarykamysh delta along the bed of the Daudan, played a major role in the religious life of the area. The majority of the religious objects and finds from the site are associated with the cult of fertility, but the economic documents recovered there indicate that religious and fiscal functions were combined. Three types of ritual structures in Khwarazm of this period are known: at Koi-Krylgan-qala, a freestanding temple evidently of a royal cult but probably of fertility in general,situated within the boundaries of an oasis; the ritual center at Kalali-gir 2, at which several temples functioned in a common system; and the round temple at Giaur 3, located in a settled community of herders  on the periphery of left-bank Khwarazm and identical in plan to the round temple at Kalali-gir 2.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1095, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 995})
