m the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": “Islam and its civilization flourished under this dynasty, a fact attested by the devoutness of the rulers, their deference to men of religion, the endowments they made to pious foundations, and the monuments of religious as well as utilitarian architecture with which they embellished their realms."  "The Karaskanid rulers maintained their stronghold in the eastern pars of Central Aaisa, in the cites of Balasagun and Kashgar. Within a few decades these cities grew into bustling urban centers of about 100,000 inhabitants, hosting numerous mosques, Christian churches and probably monasteries and Islamic madrasas.“ [...]“ The Karakhanid era signifies important changes in Turkic culture, including the formation of Muslim Turkic identity and the codification of the Turkic cultural legacy in the 1070s.“  “In the mid-tenth century, the Karakhanids themselves adopted Islam and declared it to be the religion of their tribal society. They began to take Muslim names and , later, Muslim honorfics (alqab; pl of laqab).“  “In the year 960, according to Ibn Miskawaih and Ibn al-athir, “200,000 tents of the Turks went over to Islam“. Athough it is nowhere specified who these “Turks“ were, circumstantial evidence points to the Karashanid dynasty and its tribal followers.“ '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 963, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 879})
