om the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": The following quote highlights the widespread use of central courts as a ritualised space within Minoan ‘palaces’ around Crete. “Graham (1957, 1962), Shaw (1973), Preziosi (1983) and others have also emphasised the repetition of the proportions and orientations followed by the Central Courts, and it seems fair to assume that this standardisation corresponds with a set of prescribed rules that one or more of the rites taking place on the court dictated […] I suspect that they were perhaps largely ecstatic, maybe drug-induced and most likely involved larger groups, so dancing and feasting are the most likely candidates. Gesell, for example, has calculated that the Central Courts of the three main palaces could have held about 1, 698 milling people or about 5, 435 people standing in a crowd.” […] “[…] I want to argue that the ritual performances that took place within the Central Court were the first unifying and integrative actions that bound society together and made ‘Minoans’ our of them. It implies, in essence, that I believe that all buildings or rooms around these courts were simply dependencies or ancillary rooms serving a variety of needs, such as administration, storage, production, residence, and cult, but that these functions remained secondary to its main and primary purpose up to the end of Late Minoan IB.” […] “The palatial courts at Petras and Monastiraki may date back to the Protopalatial period; but the cement paved 40 by 15m large public court at Gournia my already have been laid out at the very end of the Prepalatial period in MM I, serving, according to Damiani Indelicato (1984, 53), as the original hub for the urban street system. Here and at Petras and Monastiraki, the courts are situated on the highest spot of the hill with immediately behind, a rocky outcrop. It is possible that such natural features formed an integral part of ritual activities taking place of these courts, as recently argued by Kanta and Tzigounaki (2001) and Davaras (1999).”  The following quote attests to similarities of sanctuary/ritual deposits found in different Old Palace Minoan sites on Crete highlighting a related system of religious belief. “The stone figures and votive limbs from room V5 constitute a unique assemblage at Malia, but can be compared to finds from settlements, caves, and peak sanctuaries elsewhere. The stylized marble figurine M67/F1 has been comparted to an almost identical piece found in the palace at Kato Zakros. Other figurines, made of limestone, have comparanda from Malia itself (Maison Epsilon) and from Trapeza in the Lasithi plain. A small dog made of steatite resembles a piece from the peak sactuary of Mt. Juktas. Jewelry and decorative items most notably fiancé beads, from V5 of building B, maybe compared to finds from the Knossian palace, such as the Vat Room Deposit and the Temple Repositories.” <br>The following quotes suggest that a standard Old Palace Minoan religion was widespread throughout the island. “Graham (1957, 1962), Shaw (1973), Preziosi (1983) and others have also emphasised the repetition of the proportions and orientations followed by the Central Courts, and it seems fair to assume that this standardisation corresponds with a set of prescribed rules that one or more of the rites taking place on the court dictated […] I suspect that they were perhaps largely ecstatic, maybe drug-induced and most likely involved larger groups, so dancing and feasting are the most likely candidates. Gesell, for example, has calculated that the Central Courts of the three main palaces could have held about 1, 698 milling people or about 5, 435 people standing in a crowd.” […] “[…] I want to argue that the ritual performances that took place within the Central Court were the first unifying and integrative actions that bound society together and made ‘Minoans’ our of them. It implies, in essence, that I believe that all buildings or rooms around these courts were simply dependencies or ancillary rooms serving a variety of needs, such as administration, storage, production, residence, and cult, but that these functions remained secondary to its main and primary purpose up to the end of Late Minoan IB.” […] “The palatial courts at Petras and Monastiraki may date back to the Protopalatial period; but the cement paved 40 by 15m large public court at Gournia my already have been laid out at the very end of the Prepalatial period in MM I, serving, according to Damiani Indelicato (1984, 53), as the original hub for the urban street system. Here and at Petras and Monastiraki, the courts are situated on the highest spot of the hill with immediately behind, a rocky outcrop. It is possible that such natural features formed an integral part of ritual activities taking place of these courts, as recently argued by Kanta and Tzigounaki (2001) and Davaras (1999).”  The stone figures and votive limbs from room V5 constitute a unique assemblage at Malia, but can be compared to finds from settlements, caves, and peak sanctuaries elsewhere. The stylized marble figurine M67/F1 has been comparted to an almost identical piece found in the palace at Kato Zakros. Other figurines, made of limestone, have comparanda from Malia itself (Maison Epsilon) and from Trapeza in the Lasithi plain. A small dog made of steatite resembles a piece from the peak sactuary of Mt. Juktas. Jewelry and decorative items most notably fiancé beads, from V5 of building B, maybe compared to finds from the Knossian palace, such as the Vat Room Deposit and the Temple Repositories.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1919, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1825})
