ed by the Mycenaeans. Nevertheless it had a shrine room complete with a Minoan-style bench, image of a goddess, votaries, and ritual symbols and equipment. During this period similar shrines began to appear in towns and settlements around the island. It is clear that these are now set up for the public, as they have larger images more suitable for display to groups of people, and that the images, ritual equipment, and offerings that were found in them are of affordable materials commonly used in daily life.” […] NB the following quote highlights the widespread use of Minoan religious shrines in settlements and towns. “By the LM III B and III C periods there is growing evidence for shrines in towns and settlements. The important new features to be considered include the location and interior arrangement of the shrine and the type of image and ritual equipment, including the size of the image and the material from which it was made. Distinct assemblages of ritual images and implements have been found on at least eight sites, along with single images or pieces of equipment on several others, and at four sites a sufficient amount of the town has been excavated to give a context for the shrine. Although most of these sites are locate in Central Crete and in the area of the Isthmus of Ierapetra, a few have been found in southern Crete and as far west as the Rethymnon district.” […] NB the following quote describes the widespread depiction of a goddess figure throughout Crete. “Whatever the images represent – goddesses, priestesses, or worshipers, it is clear that the practices and rituals associated with the Minoan snake goddess from the Temple Repositories in the palace at Knossos evolved into a popular religion, with rituals performed at least partly in open areas in front of shrines and temples in many, perhaps all, of the towns of Postpalatial Crete.” <br>The following quote suggests that Minoan religion continued to survive in some capacity during LM II -LM III B per