presenting Harappan “religious” beliefs and practices – seals, horned male deities, mother goddess figurines, and fire altars, among others – but all attempts to correlate these objects to Indian mythology, religion or contemporary Mesopotamian religious beliefs have suffered from the lack of deciphered text (Allchin 1985; Ashfaque, 1989; Dhavalikar and Atre, 1989; Fairservis, 1975; 1984; Parpola, 1984; 1988)” <br>We know that there are consistent pockets of immigrants living in Kachi Plain during the Indus Civilization, mainly due to trade, which may be adherent to other religious beliefs. Thus, it is plausible to suggest that the adherents to the most widespread religion [the Harappan religion] cannot be the totality. But given that it is difficult to find evidence of religion, and all the evidence points toward a unified religious belief system, it is plausible to envision that the vast majority of people would be Harappan Religion believers. “Massive population sizes in the latter half of the second millennium derive from high levels of immigration, including people who then plied their occupational specializations in city’s enclaves. […] However, we have no clear idea about the composition of the Harappan population. […] A preliminary analysis of stable isotopes [from the skeletons] suggested a heterogeneous population in the urban period cemetery R-37 at Harappa.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1140, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 996})
