ictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": The following quote estimates the percent of Paris Huguenots between 1-2%, implying Catholics represented around 98% of the population as the only other religious minority I have identified is a very small group of Jewish individuals. “The absence of formal structures was compounded by the minority status of the Paris Huguenots. Even on the most generous estimates they represented no more than 1–2 per cent of the population of Paris, perhaps 4,000 at the end of the seventeenth century in a total population around half a million. By 1789, when the city contained around 700,000 inhabitants, one historian has estimated that there were 5,000–6,000 Reformed Protestants. I have identified over 5,000 individuals, representing all social groups except the nobility and exercising a very wide range of occupations, who at some time between 1700 and 1789 indicated their commitment to the Reformed faith.”  “Under the Edict of Nantes of 1598, designed to end religious warfare in France, the towns of France were classified as Catholic, Protestant or mixed, and the rights and privileges of each religious community were laid out. Paris was designated a Catholic city, which meant that Protestants could live and work there but not practise their religion publicly. For baptisms, marriages and funerals, and of course regular Sunday worship, they had to go to Charenton. They also had to pay ecclesiastical taxes, could not work on Catholic feast days and were not permitted to marry during Lent.”   “Protestantism was illegal in eighteenth-century France, yet many French Reformed Protestants, better known as Huguenots, managed to maintain their religion and identity until the French Revolution granted religious freedom. Several thousand of them lived in Paris, but remained a tiny minority in a very Catholic city.”  NB The percentage range estimate has been further broadened to take into account the presence of indigenous religions in the French Empire more broadly.'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1090, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 999})
