ion from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: ditch\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of ditch as a military technology used in warfare.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was the use of ditches as a military technology present or absent in the warfare strategies of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Variable name: chain\n"Variable description: The absence or presence of chainmail as a military technology used in warfare. We’re using a broad definition of chainmail. Habergeon was the word used to describe the Chinese version and that would qualify as chainmail. Armor that is made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh.'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "During the period from <time-start> to <time-end>, was chainmail, defined as armor made of small metal rings linked together in a pattern to form a mesh, present or absent as a military technology in the warfare practices of '<polity>'? Strictly choose an option from the unordered set {A, B}. A = present, B = absent"}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '"Variable name": Widespread Religion\n"Variable description": ’" The first quote suggests that the polytheistic Elamite religion is the most widespread religion of Elam. “The information furnished by archeological excavations in Persia and by cuneiform documents permit a summary description of some aspects of Elamite religion from the end of the 3rd millennium B.C.E. until the Achaemenid period…In texts from Susa written in Sumerian, Akkadian, or Elamite (see vii, below) and in Mesopotamian documents relating to Elam more than 200 divinities are mentioned as having been honored in Susiana and on the Persian plateau. This total results from several factors.” <br>‘" While there is limited information on the prevalence of religion in the Shimashki Dynasty, the following quote suggests that the polytheistic Elamite religion was prevalent across all regions of Elam, with worship of particular gods varying across regions. “From the corpus of Elamite, Sumerian and Akkadian texts we know some 200 Elamite gods (Vallat 1998: 335). Some of them were indigenous to Elam, whereas some others had their roots in Mesopotamia and other neighboring regions. Although some of them were prominent gods and were worshiped in most of the Elamite cities, others were worshiped only locally. Many names in the list are gods whose names are known to us only as components in theophoric names. Moreover, it seems that although certain gods and goddesses were honored throughout the history of Elam, many were worshiped only in certain periods.” '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1021, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 923})
