ear that, traditionally in India, people readily transferred or distributed their allegiance between different sects, seeing no logical inconsistency in approaching different gods for different purposes, and that this apparently syncretic style of religious behaviour encouraged a relaxed attitude to what others did as well; evidently, too, rulers generally extended their acceptance of this practice.”<br>The following suggests that the majority of the population remained Hindu. “To contrast the nature of British colonial state’s association and handling of temples it would be interesting to look into the example of Nawabs of Arcot’s association with temples. T.V. Mahalingam brings out the interest evinced by the Nawabs of Arcot in Hindu Temples. Illustrating with a few instances of the Nawabs’ involvement in the Hindu temples he shows how they resolved the disputes in administration and rituals of Hindu temples. Interestingly while the Nawab’s understood the significance of temples as social and economic institutions. However they did not reformulated the Hindu laws and customs but rather attempted to preserve the tradition as such. In the words of T.V. Mahalingam,  ‘The Nawabs of the Carnatic (the members of the Walajah dynasty of Arcot), particularly Muhammad Ali Walajah and his successor Umdata’I Umara, were deeply interested in the proper maintenance of the Hindu temples. They were anxious that no harm was done to them in the course of the many wars that were waged in the Carnatic during their period. More than that, they were equally anxious that the traditional and customary modes of worship there were followed without any interruption. They considered it their duty to settle disputes, if any, that arose among groups or parties with regard to the performance of temple ritual and worship.”  NB Though sources distinguish between Saivism and Vaisnavism, the following suggests that the majority of the population followed both, and indeed likely other Hindu sects as well. “It seems fairly clear that, traditionally in India, people readily transferred or distributed their allegiance between different sects, seeing no logical inconsistency in approaching different gods for different purposes, and that this apparently syncretic style of religious behaviour encouraged a relaxed attitude to what others did as well; evidently, too, rulers generally extended their acceptance of this practice.”'}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1280, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1196})
